{"text":"### Logical Deduction: Stack vs. Queue\n\nTo understand the difference between a stack and a queue, we must look at how they handle the order of data processing. Both are linear data structures, but they operate on opposite principles regarding the sequence of insertion and removal.\n\n#### 1. The Stack (LIFO)\nA **Stack** operates on the principle of **LIFO** (Last-In, First-Out). Imagine a stack of plates in a cafeteria: you place a new plate on top, and when you need a plate, you take the one from the top. The last plate placed is the first one removed.","output_ids":[13962,59977,48704,2567,25,13875,5972,13,18182,271,1206,3418,279,6463,1881,264,5436,321,264,6951,11,567,1902,1353,506,1204,781,3579,279,1906,314,795,8427,13,11396,513,13094,795,13976,11,694,781,14061,383,13600,15694,8559,279,8240,314,34736,321,16162,13,271,794,220,16,13,561,13875,318,43,25166,8,198,32,2972,4195,332,25224,383,279,16988,314,2972,43,25166,332,318,5661,30463,11,5339,81600,553,36541,264,5436,314,23702,303,264,90613,25,488,1925,264,491,11629,383,1844,11,321,948,488,1144,264,11629,11,488,1831,279,799,494,279,1844,13,561,1483,11629,8826,369,279,1118,799,6747,13],"meta_info":{"id":"ea9e2d410d694b4da74daf92d1fdd7c3","finish_reason":{"type":"length","length":128},"prompt_tokens":29,"weight_version":"default","num_retractions":0,"input_token_logprobs":[],"output_token_logprobs":[[-0.7143884897232056,13962,null],[-0.6405277252197266,59977,null],[-0.40027928352355957,48704,null],[-0.0003965306677855551,2567,null],[-0.8073040246963501,25,null],[-0.7325709462165833,13875,null],[-0.0010832638945430517,5972,null],[-0.0007656264933757484,13,null],[-0.0001461399078834802,18182,null],[-0.0006515049026347697,271,null],[-0.1877610981464386,1206,null],[-0.020467272028326988,3418,null],[-0.00019107422849629074,279,null],[-0.37465134263038635,6463,null],[-0.025252774357795715,1881,null],[-0.05582105740904808,264,null],[-0.870582103729248,5436,null],[-0.00017152745567727834,321,null],[-0.0002685424697